Implement and Benchmark Advanced Code Splitting Strategies in a React Application
Objective
Build a multi-page React application to implement and benchmark four distinct code-splitting strategies: none, route-based, component-based, and library-based. You will learn to use React.lazy, Suspense, and Vite's manualChunks to optimize application performance. This task is crucial for developing production-grade web applications that deliver fast initial load times and efficient resource caching.

Description
1. Project Overview & Core Concepts
Modern web applications have grown increasingly complex, often leading to large JavaScript bundles that can significantly slow down initial page loads. A slow start can frustrate users and negatively impact key business metrics. The primary technique to combat this is code splitting, a process of dividing the application's code into smaller, more manageable chunks that can be loaded on demand.

In this project, you will build a React application from the ground up and systematically apply and benchmark four different code-splitting strategies. Your goal is not just to implement these techniques but to deeply understand their impact on performance, caching, and user experience. You will use industry-standard tools to measure bundle size, analyze network waterfalls, and quantify improvements in key metrics like Time to Interactive (TTI).

Key Technologies and Concepts
React (React.lazy and Suspense): React provides built-in tools for code splitting. React.lazy lets you render a dynamically imported component as a regular component. It works seamlessly with Suspense, which allows you to specify a loading indicator (a fallback UI) while the lazy component's code is being loaded over the network.
Why it matters: This is the idiomatic way to implement route-based and component-based splitting in React. It integrates directly with the React component model, making it declarative and easy to reason about. A common pitfall is forgetting that React.lazy currently only supports default exports.
Dynamic import(): This is the underlying JavaScript language feature that makes code splitting possible. Unlike a static import statement, which bundles the module at build time, a dynamic import() is a function-like expression that returns a Promise. The module is fetched only when the import() is called, allowing for on-demand loading.
Why it matters: It's the engine behind React.lazy. Understanding how it works will help you debug issues and even implement custom lazy-loading logic if needed.
Vite (manualChunks): Vite is a modern, fast build tool for web development. Its configuration allows for fine-grained control over the build output. The build.rollupOptions.output.manualChunks option lets you define rules to group specific modules into custom chunks. This is particularly useful for separating large, stable third-party libraries (vendor code) from your more frequently changing application code.
Why it matters: This strategy dramatically improves caching. When you deploy a small change to your app, users only need to re-download the small app code chunk, not the entire vendor library, which their browser has likely cached from a previous visit.
Performance Benchmarking (Lighthouse & Chrome DevTools): You can't optimize what you can't measure. Lighthouse is an automated tool for auditing web page quality, providing scores for performance, accessibility, and more. The Chrome DevTools Network tab provides a waterfall view of all resources loaded by a page, showing what is being loaded, when, and from where (network or cache).
Why it matters: These tools provide the objective data needed to prove that your optimizations are working. They help you move from "I think this is faster" to "I have reduced the initial JS payload by 150KB and improved TTI by 400ms."


