3. Implementation Step-by-Step: Building the Four Versions
Follow these steps to create each version of the application.

Version 1: The Monolith (Baseline)
This version serves as your performance baseline. All code is bundled into a single file.

Project Setup: Initialize a new React project using Vite.

Install Dependencies: Add react-router-dom, chart.js, @tiptap/react, @tiptap/starter-kit, and other necessary libraries.

Create Pages: Build the four page components (Dashboard, Analytics, Editor, Settings). Ensure the Analytics page renders a Chart.js chart and the Editor page renders a TipTap instance.

Set Up Routing: In your main App.jsx, use static imports for all page components and configure the routes using React Router.

// App.jsx - Version 1
import Dashboard from './pages/Dashboard';
import Analytics from './pages/Analytics';
import Editor from './pages/Editor';
import Settings from './pages/Settings';

// ... Router setup with <Routes> and <Route> ...
Build: Run your build:nosplit script. Inspect the output directory (dist). You should see one large JavaScript file containing all your application code and dependencies.

Version 2: Route-Based Splitting
This is the most common and impactful form of code splitting.

Refactor Imports: Modify App.jsx to use React.lazy and dynamic import() for each page component.

// App.jsx - Version 2
import { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';

const Dashboard = lazy(() => import('./pages/Dashboard'));
const Analytics = lazy(() => import('./pages/Analytics'));
// ... etc.
Add Suspense: Wrap your <Routes> component with a <Suspense> boundary. Provide a fallback UI, such as a simple loading spinner or a page skeleton, to show while the route's chunk is loading.

<Suspense fallback={<div>Loading page...</div>}>
  <Routes>
    {/* ... Route definitions ... */}
  </Routes>
</Suspense>
Build: Run your build:routes script. The output directory should now contain multiple smaller JavaScript chunks, one for each lazily loaded page, in addition to the main entry chunk.

Version 3: Component-Based Splitting
Here, you'll apply splitting to a heavy component within a page.

Create a Heavy Component: On your Dashboard page, create a QuickReportModal component that uses Chart.js. This modal will be shown only when a user clicks a button.

Implement Lazy Loading: In the Dashboard component, lazily import the QuickReportModal. Use a state variable to control its visibility.

// Dashboard.jsx - Version 3
const QuickReportModal = lazy(() => import('../components/QuickReportModal'));

function Dashboard() {
  const [showModal, setShowModal] = useState(false);
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setShowModal(true)}>View Quick Report</button>
      {showModal && (
        <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading report...</div>}>
          <QuickReportModal onClose={() => setShowModal(false)} />
        </Suspense>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
Build: Run your build:components script. You should see a new, separate chunk specifically for QuickReportModal.

Version 4: Library Splitting (Manual Chunks)
This final version optimizes for caching by separating vendor code.

Configure Vite: Create a vite.config.libsplit.js file. Use the manualChunks option to define rules for splitting out large libraries.

// vite.config.libsplit.js
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  build: {
    rollupOptions: {
      output: {
        manualChunks: (id) => {
          if (id.includes('node_modules/chart.js')) {
            return 'vendor-chartjs';
          }
          if (id.includes('node_modules/@tiptap')) {
            return 'vendor-tiptap';
          }
          if (id.includes('node_modules/react')) {
            return 'vendor-react';
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
});
Build: Run the build:libsplit script. Inspect the build output. You will now see chunks named vendor-chartjs.js, vendor-tiptap.js, and vendor-react.js, completely separate from your application code chunks.

