6. FAQ
Q: My React.lazy() import is failing with an error about a component not being a valid React component. What's wrong?
A: This almost always happens because React.lazy requires the imported file to have a default export. If your component is a named export (e.g., export function MyComponent), it will fail. You can either change it to export default function MyComponent or use an adapter in the import statement: const MyComponent = lazy(() => import('./MyComponent').then(module => ({ default: module.MyComponent })));. The former is generally cleaner. See the React documentation for more details.
Q: Why do I need four separate Vite config files?
A: While you could use environment variables to control a single config, separate files provide maximum clarity and isolation for this exercise. It ensures that the build configuration for one strategy does not accidentally interfere with another, making your benchmarking results clean and reliable.
Q: The manualChunks configuration seems complex. How do I know what to include in the string?
A: The id passed to the manualChunks function is the absolute file path of the module being processed. The simplest way to create a vendor chunk is to check if the path includes 'node_modules/library-name'. You can use a bundle analyzer tool like vite-bundle-visualizer to see which libraries are contributing the most to your bundle size, which helps you decide what to split out. Refer to the Vite documentation for advanced patterns.
